From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 06:00:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] iommu: apple-dart: let a sleep leave idle DARTs to
 their runtime resume

After a real sleep, apple_dart_resume_noirq() also writes back the state
of a DART that runtime PM had suspended, in case its consumer's firmware
uses it before anything resumes it at runtime. That restore reads the
DART first, in noirq, before the host whose clock or setup the DART's
block may need has resumed. On the J604 the first deep-sleep resumes
died on exactly such a read: a PCIe DART, powered, in a port nobody had
clocked again.

Add wake_restore_suspended. On, the default, keeps today's behaviour.
Off, a DART runtime PM had suspended is left alone in noirq: its runtime
resume restores it before its next user, as at any runtime power-up.
That is the variant to try when a resume stops at a DART read whose
host comes back later.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/iommu/apple-dart.c | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/iommu/apple-dart.c b/drivers/iommu/apple-dart.c
index de28ee8695..3176df5265 100644
--- a/drivers/iommu/apple-dart.c
+++ b/drivers/iommu/apple-dart.c
@@ -6551,6 +6551,16 @@ static __maybe_unused int apple_dart_resume(struct device *dev)
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Off: after a real sleep, a DART runtime PM had suspended is left to its runtime resume, which
+ * restores it before its next user, instead of being written in noirq while the block its
+ * host sets up (a port clock, a coprocessor) may not answer yet. On: as before.
+ */
+static bool wake_restore_suspended = true;
+module_param(wake_restore_suspended, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(wake_restore_suspended,
+		 "After a real sleep, restore runtime-suspended DARTs in noirq (default) or at their runtime resume");
+
 static __maybe_unused int apple_dart_resume_noirq(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct apple_dart *dart = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
@@ -6580,7 +6590,7 @@ static __maybe_unused int apple_dart_resume_noirq(struct device *dev)
 	 * starting its firmware, and this would land on top.
 	 */
 	if (!ret && pm_resume_via_firmware() && pm_runtime_status_suspended(dev) &&
-	    !dart->sleep_restore.count) {
+	    !dart->sleep_restore.count && READ_ONCE(wake_restore_suspended)) {
 		ret = apple_dart_resume(dev);
 		if (ret)
 			dev_err(dev, "restore after sleep failed: %d\n", ret);
-- 
2.43.0

